{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the charge of Elazar son of Aharon the priest: the oil of the luminary</b> — He says that he is the appointee over all of these, and his charge includes the charge of the entire Tabernacle and the charge of all that is in it. And Rabbi Avraham (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 4:16) said that its meaning is that he has a charge also over the Tabernacle and all its vessels together with Itamar his brother, only for the greatness of Elazar's rank he alone was appointee over the oil and over the incense. But this is not correct, for Scripture says (verses 28, 33) \"by the hand of Itamar,\" and it explained here \"the holy and its vessels.\" And so He said above: \"And the prince of the princes of the Levites, Elazar son of Aharon the priest, the charge of those who keep the charge of the sanctuary\" (3:32) — meaning, the prince of the charge of the guard of the sanctuary, which are those given into the hand of the sons of Kehat. Rather, the meaning of \"the charge of the entire Tabernacle\" is that the charge of the oil of the luminary and the incense and the meal-offering and the anointing oil, and the charge of the entire Tabernacle in the sanctuary and all that is in it among the holy vessels — everything is in the hand of Elazar. And these are those mentioned here (Numbers 4:15), where He said: \"And Aharon and his sons shall finish covering the sanctuary and all the vessels of the sanctuary.\" Behold, Elazar was the steward over the three princes and the treasurer for the guard of the sons of Kehat, and Itamar was treasurer for Gershon and Merari. And because Scripture did not mention regarding Elazar \"and he shall carry\" as is said regarding the Levites, it would appear that he does not carry them; rather, he is the appointee over them and gives them from his hand into the hand of the sons of Kehat whom he chooses among them with zeal and piety, and they carry them and return them to his hand when the Tabernacle is set up. And so too \"the charge of those who keep the charge of the sanctuary\" (Numbers 3:32), and so too \"by their names you shall count the vessels of the charge of their burden\" (Numbers 4:32). And according to the opinion of the Yerushalmi (Shabbat 10:3), cited in Rashi's commentaries (Shabbat 92a, s.v. שכן), that he does carry them, the burden would be very great, for the incense is 365 maneh (Keritot 6a), and Moshe our teacher did not pack it in halves, and the oil of the luminary for a full year is very plentiful — 173 log, and the meal-offering of the daily sacrifice — we do not know for how many days he would carry of it. But Elazar was strong and mighty like Yaakov our father, and so too Moshe our teacher and Aharon his brother, and \"those who hope in HaShem shall renew strength\" (Isaiah 40:31)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The quotation in Rashi is identified as שבת צב (Shabbat 92a); the Yerushalmi citation שבת פ״י ה״ג corresponds to Shabbat 10:3. The heading 'ד״ה שכן' resolves to the standard s.v. abbreviation for the Rashi gloss beginning with that word."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:44.490800269Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}